   "source": {
    "label": "thm:reg1",
    "number": "Theorem 3.2",
    "quote": "Let $\\delta \\in (0, 1)$ and suppose $f$ is sampled from $\\mathrm{GP}(0, \\ntk)$. Values of $f$ are observed with a zero-mean Gaussian noise of variance $\\sigma^2$, and the exploration parameter is set to $\\beta_t = 2 \\log (\\vert\\mathcal{A} \\vert t^2 \\pi^2/6\\delta)$. Then with probability greater than $1-\\delta$, the regret of \\ntkalg satisfies $R_T \\leq C \\sqrt{T\\beta_T\\gamma_T}$ for any $T\\geq 1$, where $C := \\sqrt{8\\sigma^{-2}/\\log (1+\\sigma^{-2})}$."
   },
   "confidence": "medium",
   "notes": "Regret is random (over GP sample and noise) and bounded w.p. 1-delta; quantity chosen as bayesian_regret since f is drawn from a prior, but bound is high-probability rather than averaged. Theorem states C=sqrt(8 sigma^{-2}/log(1+sigma^{-2})); proof gives sqrt(8 sigma^2/...); recorded as stated."
  },
